<DocsLayout title="Caching">
  <p>
    The API caches heavily, which keeps upstream requests down and responses fast.
    Knowing how the cache works helps you tune your integration and read the response headers.
  </p>

  <h2>Cache types</h2>

  <h3>Positive cache</h3>
  <p>
    Lyrics that are found stay cached until someone clears them manually.
    Positive cache entries include the TTML data, track duration, and match score.
  </p>

  <h3>Negative cache</h3>
  <p>
    When lyrics are <em>not</em> found, that result is cached too, so the API does not repeat the lookup.
    Negative cache entries expire after 7 days by default.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you receive a <code>NEGATIVE_HIT</code> response and believe lyrics should exist,
    use the <code>/revalidate</code> endpoint (requires API key) to force a fresh lookup.
  </p>

  <h2>Cache key format</h2>
  <p><code>/getLyrics</code> normalizes cache keys so equivalent queries share an entry:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">{`ttml_lyrics:{song} {artist} {album} {duration}s`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>All text is converted to lowercase</li>
    <li>Leading/trailing whitespace is trimmed</li>
    <li>Album and duration are left out when they are not sent. They are optional but improve cache hit rates</li>
    <li><code>/getLyrics</code> also accepts a cached entry whose duration differs by a small amount. The provider-specific endpoints need an exact match</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For example, searching for "Shape of You" by "Ed Sheeran" and "shape of you" by "ed sheeran"
    hit the same cache entry.
  </p>

  <h2>Cache status header</h2>
  <p>Responses from the lyrics endpoints include an <code>X-Cache-Status</code> header:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Value</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>HIT</code></td>
        <td>Lyrics served from cache</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>MISS</code></td>
        <td>Not in the cache. The lyrics were fetched fresh and cached, or the request failed</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>NEGATIVE_HIT</code></td>
        <td>Previously searched, lyrics not found (cached negative result)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>STALE</code></td>
        <td>Backend failed, serving older cached version as fallback. Only on <code>/getLyrics</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why duration matters</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>duration</code> parameter improves both cache hit rates and match accuracy:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps distinguish between different versions of the same song (radio edit vs album version)</li>
    <li>Improves matching when song/artist names have variations</li>
    <li>Makes cache keys more specific, so there are fewer false positives</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your application has access to track duration (e.g., from a music player),
    always include it in your requests.
  </p>

  <h2>Cache fallback behavior</h2>
  <p>
    When the upstream fetch for <code>/getLyrics</code> fails, the API tries to serve stale cached data:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Primary cache key lookup fails (fresh fetch attempted)</li>
    <li>Upstream provider returns error</li>
    <li>If an album was sent, the API checks the cache for the same query without the album</li>
    <li>If found, serves stale cache with <code>X-Cache-Status: STALE</code></li>
    <li>If not found, returns the original error</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Revalidation</h2>
  <p>
    To force a cache refresh, use the <a href="/reference/revalidate"><code>/revalidate</code></a> endpoint (requires API key).
    It refreshes only entries that already exist, and only the default TTML entry that <code>/getLyrics</code> serves:
  </p>
  <CodeTabs tabs={[
    { language: 'curl', code: `curl -H "X-API-Key: your-api-key" \\
  "https://api.betterlyrics.org/revalidate?s=Song&a=Artist"` },
    { language: 'javascript', code: `const res = await fetch("https://api.betterlyrics.org/revalidate?s=Song&a=Artist", {
  headers: { "X-API-Key": "your-api-key" },
});
const data = await res.json();` },
    { language: 'python', code: `import requests

res = requests.get(
    "https://api.betterlyrics.org/revalidate?s=Song&a=Artist",
    headers={"X-API-Key": "your-api-key"},
)
data = res.json()` },
    { language: 'go', code: `package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	req, err := http.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, "https://api.betterlyrics.org/revalidate?s=Song&a=Artist", nil)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	req.Header.Set("X-API-Key", "your-api-key")

	res, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer res.Body.Close()

	body, err := io.ReadAll(res.Body)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(string(body))
}` },
  ]} />
  <p>
    Use it when:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You know lyrics have been corrected at the source</li>
    <li>A negative cache entry should be re-checked</li>
    <li>You want to verify the cache is up-to-date</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Client-side caching</h2>
  <p>
    Cache on the client too, to cut down on API calls:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Cache successful responses locally for the session or longer</li>
    <li>Use the song+artist+duration as your local cache key</li>
    <li>Respect <code>NEGATIVE_HIT</code>: don't retry songs without lyrics right away</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
